# b24_string_escapes.test — issue #400 Stage 1: what a LogQL double-quoted
# string DECODES to. The `eval` rows pin it by the LINES a query returns;
# the `eval_fail` rows pin the escapes the grammar refuses.
#
# Every expected label set and line below is a byte-exact capture from
# grafana/loki:3.7.4, image digest
# sha256:87f0a067673756a3cede1bcbf0c74875f7df9b09fddb53e399d0c576f756cfcc,
# booted on `ci/logql/config.yaml` with no delta of its own;
# `detected_level` stripped per PROVENANCE.md. **Two containers from that
# one digest, and the split is stated because the header used to name
# only the first:** `pulsus-400s1-loki` captured the `esc` and `escc`
# sections, `pulsus-400s1-ci` the `ctl` section, which was added a round
# later. Container identity READ FROM THE RUNNING PROCESS, not from this
# header: /loki/api/v1/status/buildinfo answered {"version":"3.7.4",
# "revision":"b318f282","branch":"release-3.7.x"} on each — the same
# b318f282 the pinned digest and the Go checkout at v3.7.4 resolve to.
# Captured 2026-08-10 over `/loki/api/v1/query_range`, every row replayed
# from the query text read back out of this file so the probe cannot
# drift from what is committed.
#
# provenance: captured
# (file default; see logqltest/PROVENANCE.md §provenance markers.
#  A row may override it with its own `# provenance:` line.)
#
# THE RULE, read from the reference rather than inferred from these rows
# (grafana/loki @ v3.7.4, b318f2829f0ae2094ab3a1e90780450e9e4b03be).
# Loki's lexer hands every string token to
# `prometheus/util/strutil.Unquote`
# (`pkg/logql/syntax/lex.go:190-201` → `util/strutil/quote.go:66-231`),
# whose `unquoteChar` defines `\a \b \f \n \r \t \v \\ \"`,
# `\xHH` (two hex, a raw BYTE), `\NNN` (exactly three octal digits, over
# 255 an error), `\uXXXX` and `\UXXXXXXXX` (over `utf8.MaxRune` an error)
# — and REFUSES everything else, including `\d`, `\q`, `\0` and `\'`.
# Two consequences this file exists for:
#
#   * `\xHH`/`\NNN` are BYTES, so consecutive escapes compose: `"\xc3\xa9"`
#     is the single character `é` (`Unquote` appends `byte(c)` for a
#     non-multibyte value, `quote.go:99-104`).
#   * a `\u`/`\U` SURROGATE is not refused — `utf8.EncodeRune` writes
#     U+FFFD for an invalid rune — so `"\ud800"` is a pattern for U+FFFD.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS ABOUT RETURNED LINES AND NOT STATUSES. Until #400
# Stage 1, `scan_double_quoted` ended in `Some(other) => value.push(other)`
# — drop the backslash, keep the character. `{app=~"\101"}` therefore
# selected the stream `101` where the reference selects `A`: **two
# disjoint answers, both 200, no error on either side.** Measured at the
# wire on the capture container, with the two streams pushed under one
# `service_name`:
#
#   {app=~"\101", service_name="esc2"} -> the `app="A"` stream and its line
#   {app=~"101",  service_name="esc2"} -> the `app="101"` stream and its line
#
# The hermetic runner does not re-apply the stream SELECTOR (§ the DSL
# note in PROVENANCE.md: an eval replays every loaded line), so the rows
# below put the same escapes in a LINE FILTER instead. **`| decolorize`
# was load-bearing and not decoration when these rows were written**: a
# line filter sitting directly on the selector is PUSHED DOWN to SQL, so
# `CompiledPipeline::compile` skips it (`pipeline.rs:1009-1014` returns
# `Ok(None)`) and this runner — which executes the pipeline and no SQL —
# returned every loaded line whatever the pattern said. Written without
# it, every row here would have passed before and after Stage 1 while
# measuring nothing. (The line citation above read `:986-996` until issue
# #278; the check had moved, and the sentence around it was rewritten at
# the same time.)
#
# **That is no longer why the `| decolorize` is here.** Issue #278 taught
# the runner to plan first and compile client-side, so a pushed-down
# filter IS applied now, and `b26_line_filter_pushdown.test` covers that
# path directly. These rows keep the spelling that was captured — the
# queries of committed rows do not change — and `| decolorize` remains a
# no-op on these bodies (no ANSI escapes), which is the same device
# `b1_parsers_filters.test` uses. The SELECTOR
# spelling is pinned instead by
# `crates/pulsus-logql/tests/string_escapes.rs`, which asserts the
# decoded matcher value as BYTES.
#
# THE SHORT ESCAPES `\a` (0x07), `\b` (0x08), `\f` (0x0C) and `\v` (0x0B)
# ARE HERE, in the `ctl` section at the end of this file, with their own
# note. An earlier draft of this header said they were deliberately
# absent because 0x0B and 0x0C are whitespace to Rust and so could not be
# written in a `load` body at all. That was wrong twice over: `trim` and
# `split_once(char::is_whitespace)` only touch the ENDS of the line and
# the FIRST whitespace run, so a decoded control byte sitting INTERIOR to
# a body survives, which is how the `ctl` bodies are written.
#
# The escapes Stage 1 does NOT change are named once, at the `ctl`
# section that would otherwise be their home. Stating them twice is how a
# qualifier goes missing from one copy.

load
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"}
  0s   A
  5s   101
  10s  x41
  15s  u0041
  20s  U00000041

# The control: the spelling that means itself still means itself, so the
# rows below are a CHANGE of answer rather than a collapse of every
# pattern onto one line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "A"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 0s A

eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "101"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 5s 101

# Go octal. The row #400 was filed on: before Stage 1 this returned the
# `101` line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\101"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 0s A

# `\xHH`. Before Stage 1 this returned the `x41` line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\x41"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 0s A

# `\uXXXX`. Before Stage 1 this returned the `u0041` line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\u0041"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 0s A

# `\UXXXXXXXX`. Before Stage 1 this returned the `U00000041` line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\U00000041"
  {app="esc", service_name="esc"} 0s A

# An escape Go does not define is a 400 at the reference's LEXER, before
# any regex parser. All four rows below answer
# `parse error at line 1, col 38: invalid char escape` — the same column,
# because the escape sits at the same offset in each. The column is the
# ONLY thing that moves when the same escape is put to another construct,
# and this figure moved once already: it read col 25 while these rows
# were still written without `| decolorize`. 38 - 25 = 13, the length of
# the inserted ` | decolorize`, which is the check that the two readings
# are the same rule and not a re-measurement gone wrong. Before Stage 1
# they were served here as the patterns `d+`, `q`, `0` and `'`.
# The `msg:` text is PulsusDB's own (only the STATUS is claimed against
# the reference — owner ruling on #246).
eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\d+"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\q"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\0"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\'"
  msg: invalid char escape

# A malformed form of an escape the grammar DOES define: too few hex
# digits, an octal value above the byte it denotes, a code point above
# `utf8.MaxRune`, and the brace form Go has no arm for. The reference
# answers `invalid char escape` for the first and last and `invalid
# syntax` for the middle two (the value checks live in `Unquote`, the
# shape checks in Go's `text/scanner`); both are 400.
eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\x4"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\400"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\U00110000"
  msg: invalid char escape

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="esc"} | decolorize |~ "\u{263A}"
  msg: invalid char escape

clear

load
  {app="escc", service_name="escc"}
  0s   café
  5s   repl�ace
  10s  plain
  15s  tail-ÿ

# Byte escapes COMPOSE. `"\xc3\xa9"` is one character, `é` — reading each
# `\xHH` as `char::from_u32(HH)` would give four bytes and match nothing
# here. Before Stage 1 the pattern was `xc3xa9` and this returned nothing.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\xc3\xa9"
  {app="escc", service_name="escc"} 0s café

eval instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\303\277"
  {app="escc", service_name="escc"} 15s tail-ÿ

# A surrogate decodes to U+FFFD rather than being refused — the half
# that differs from `pulsus-traceql`'s otherwise identical scanner.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\ud800"
  {app="escc", service_name="escc"} 5s repl�ace

eval instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\udfff"
  {app="escc", service_name="escc"} 5s repl�ace

# The one place this narrows the reference (ledgered
# `logql-string-escape-non-utf8`): a literal whose decoded BYTES are not
# valid UTF-8. At a line filter the reference refuses it too — `error
# parsing regexp: invalid UTF-8` — so this row is parity; the divergence
# is at its five `NewFastRegexMatcher` positions, where it answers 200
# and nothing (measured: `{app=~"\xff", service_name="esc"}` is 200 with
# an empty result), and where no line in this store could match a 0xFF
# byte anyway.
#
# RE-MEASURING THESE TWO NEEDS A RECENT WINDOW. They are a pipeline-BUILD
# error on the reference, so it answers 400 over a window it executes and
# 200 over a stale one — the trap this issue was filed with. Verified
# both ways on a fresh container: over a window ending at now they are
# `400 parse error : stage '|~ "\xff"' : error parsing regexp: invalid
# UTF-8`, and over a window months old they are 200. The lexer refusals
# above are NOT window-sensitive — they answer col 38 in either window,
# which is what "raised before any regex parser" means at the wire.
eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\xff"
  msg: not valid UTF-8

eval_fail instant at 60s {service_name="escc"} | decolorize |~ "\x41\xff"
  msg: not valid UTF-8

clear

load
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"}
  0s   x07--end
  5s   x08--end
  10s  x0c--end
  15s  x0b--end
  20s  letters-abfv-end

# The short escapes `\a \b \f \v` — `quote.go:150-164`, decoding to
# 0x07 0x08 0x0C 0x0B. Before Stage 1 the lexer kept the LETTER, so each
# of these four filters was the pattern `a`, `b`, `f` or `v` and returned
# the `letters-abfv-end` line. That is what the control line is for: the
# old reading produced a different NON-EMPTY answer, so each row below is
# a change of answer rather than a fall to nothing.
#
# The four decoded bytes sit INTERIOR to their bodies deliberately: the
# DSL reads `<offset> <body>` off a `trim`ed line, and 0x0B and 0x0C are
# whitespace to Rust, so a leading or trailing one would be eaten.
#
# `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\\` and `\"` are absent, and that IS deliberate:
# Stage 1 does not change them — the old arm already decoded the first
# three and passed the last two through as themselves — so a row for any
# of them would pass before and after the fix and measure nothing. A
# decoded `\n` could not be written in a body in any case: it ends the
# line.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\a"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 0s x07--end

eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\b"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 5s x08--end

eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\f"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 10s x0c--end

eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\v"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 15s x0b--end

# The control: the LETTERS the old lexer produced still select the line
# that carries them, so the four rows above moved rather than stopped
# matching.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "abfv"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 20s letters-abfv-end

# The same bytes spelled as hex and octal escapes reach the same lines —
# one grammar, not a short-escape special case.
eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\x07"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 0s x07--end

eval instant at 60s {service_name="ctl"} | decolorize |~ "\013"
  {app="ctl", service_name="ctl"} 15s x0b--end

clear
